From 0018b1c2d3e4f5061728394a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 22:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CUDA/HIP: run the MoE shared expert's gate/up as a ninth
 channel of the routed gate+up+GLU fusion

Patch 0017 folds the shared expert's DOWN projection into the routed down
launch. Its gate/up pair is the same opportunity one step earlier. On
Laguna-XS-2.1 the routed and the shared gate/up are both a [2048 -> 512] Q4_K
matvec, they read the SAME activation (laguna.cpp hands one `cur` to both), and
the routed pair already runs eight channels of exactly that work as llama.cpp's
fused gate+up+GLU mmvq. The shared pair becomes channel nine of that launch,
which also applies its SwiGLU in-kernel.

Measured on gfx1201 (rocprofv3, per MoE layer, batch 1):

  routed gate+up+GLU, 8 channels          19.44 us
  routed gate+up+GLU, 9 channels          21.56 us   +2.12
  shared gate/up as its own grouped mmvq   4.74 us   removed
  shared SwiGLU + q8_1 fold (patch 0010)   1.39 us   removed
  q8_1 of the shared hidden, now separate   1.36 us   added
                                                    -------
                                                     -2.45 us/layer

The ninth channel costs about an eighth of the routed launch, while the pair's
own launch costs twice that, for the same reason patch 0017 recorded on the down
projection: these matvecs are memory LATENCY bound, so a launch large enough to
keep waves in flight absorbs more work almost for free while a small one cannot.
The shared expert's SwiGLU disappears into the fused kernel, but its hidden
activation then has to be quantized to q8_1 for the down projection on its own,
which is why one launch comes back; the net is one fewer dispatch per MoE layer.

The shared triple is emitted BEFORE the routed one - patch 0017 put it there so
the down fold finds the hidden activation ready - and a tensor must not be
written before ggml-alloc considers it live. So the triple is not moved. It is
DEFERRED: skipped at its own position and issued by the routed launch further
down the graph. Its output buffer is live across that whole span (allocated at
its own node, freed only after the shared down projection reads it) and nothing
in between reads it, so moving the write later is safe. Moving it earlier is not,
and that asymmetry is why no graph reordering is needed here at all: this patch
touches only the CUDA backend. Both graph arrangements tried for the down fold
cost more than they saved, one of them by silently breaking patch 0004's Q/K
grouped launch; that launch is verified still intact at 27 dispatches/token.

The skip and the fold are one decision, taken once at the shared triple and
recorded, rather than two predicates that could disagree and leave the shared
expert uncomputed. The fold runs ahead of every "already issued" check and ahead
of ggml_cuda_try_fuse, so nothing else can claim the routed node first.

Every row keeps the thread geometry, k-block assignment, vec_dot sequence and
reduction order it has in its own launch, so the result is bit-identical rather
than merely equivalent. Restricted to RDNA because the extra-channel launch
bypasses mul_mat_vec_q's small_k selection, which is unconditionally off there;
where it can be on, taking this path could move the ROUTED rows between two
thread geometries and change their bit pattern.

Measured on the R9700 (gfx1201), llama-bench tg128, five interleaved whole
process launches per arm with rotating order, median of per-round ratios:

  candidate vs series 0001-0017   1.02067  (+2.07 pct, min 1.01948 max 1.02217)
  no-op control vs series         1.00138  (+0.14 pct, min 1.00048 max 1.00583)
  candidate vs its own control    1.01866  (+1.87 pct, min 1.01624 max 1.02016)
  prefill pp512 vs series         0.99783  (-0.22 pct, min 0.99715 max 1.01110)

The control is the same binary with GGML_MOE_SHEXP_GU_FUSE=0; it lands on the
reference within the 0.6 pct noise floor, which is what isolates the change. An
independent five-round repeat before the final rebuild gave 1.02144 / 1.00156 /
1.01810 - the same result. Prefill cannot be affected: the pairing requires a
single dst column, so it never fires above batch 1.

Kernel level (rocprofv3, per decoded token): 5.1154 -> 5.0351 ms of kernel time
(-1.57 pct) and 858.1 -> 819.1 dispatches, exactly one removed per MoE layer.
Dispatch counts were checked per kernel group, not only in aggregate.

Perplexity over the fixed corpus is unchanged at 5.2611 for the reference, the
candidate and the control.

GGML_MOE_SHEXP_GU_FUSE=0 disables the fold, for A/B on a single binary.
